===== STATUS: success =====
Question: What methods improve retrieval-augmented generation, including at test time?

Self-RAG is a framework that improves retrieval-augmented generation through adaptive on-demand retrieval and self-reflection (asai2023selfraglearningto pages 1-2). Unlike conventional RAG systems that indiscriminately retrieve a fixed number of passages, Self-RAG trains a language model to generate special reflection tokens that dynamically indicate when retrieval is necessary and evaluate generation quality (asai2023selfraglearningto pages 1-2).

The framework employs two primary token mechanisms. Retrieval tokens enable the model to call a retriever on-demand, while the system processes multiple retrieved passages in parallel (asai2023selfraglearningto pages 1-2). Critique tokens allow the model to evaluate and select the best outputs (asai2023selfraglearningto pages 1-2).

At test time, reflection tokens provide controllability, enabling the model to tailor its behavior to diverse task requirements (asai2023selfraglearningto pages 1-2). This runtime controllability represents a key advancement, as it allows the system to adapt to varying task specifications without retraining.

Additionally, Self-RAG provides citations for each generated segment with self-assessment of whether outputs are supported by retrieved passages, thereby improving fact verification and factuality (asai2023selfraglearningto pages 1-2). This citation mechanism enhances verifiability by explicitly linking generated content to source material.

The combination of adaptive retrieval, parallel passage processing, and self-critique mechanisms represents a significant advancement over fixed-retrieval RAG approaches, particularly in enhancing both the verifiability and factual accuracy of generated outputs.

References

1. (asai2023selfraglearningto pages 1-2): Akari Asai, Zeqiu Wu, Yizhong Wang, Avirup Sil, and Hannaneh Hajishirzi. Self-rag: learning to retrieve, generate, and critique through self-reflection. arXiv (Cornell University), Oct 2023. URL: https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2310.11511, doi:10.48550/arxiv.2310.11511. This article has 1091 citations.
